Day 295: Largest Submatrix With Rearrangements

Problem Statement #1757

Problem Statement Explanation:

Given a binary matrix matrix of size m x n, you are allowed to rearrange the columns of the matrix in any order.
Return the area of the largest submatrix within matrix where every element of the submatrix is 1 after reordering the columns optimally.

Solution Approach:

The core idea is to treat each row as the bottom of a potential rectangle. Since we can rearrange columns freely, we only care about how many consecutive 1s are available above each cell in a given row.

1. Preprocessing Heights
We iterate through the matrix from top to bottom. 
For each cell matrix[i][j], if the value is 1, we update it to be the count of consecutive 1s ending at that cell:
If matrix[i][j] == 1, then matrix[i][j] = matrix[i-1][j] + 1.
If matrix[i][j] == 0, it remains 0.

2. Greedy Rearrangement
For each row, we now have a set of "heights." To maximize the area of a submatrix ending at this row:

Sort the heights in the current row in descending order (or ascending and iterate backward).
By sorting, we ensure that we can form a rectangle of height h with a width equal to the number of columns that have a height ≥h.
Calculate the area for each possible width: Area=height×width.

3. Global Maximum
Track the largest area found across all rows and all possible column combinations.

Here's the Code:

import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int largestSubmatrix(int[][] matrix) {
        int rows = matrix.length;
        int cols = matrix[0].length;
        int maxArea = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < rows; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {
                if (matrix[i][j] == 1) {
                    matrix[i][j] += matrix[i - 1][j];
                }
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
            int[] currentRow = matrix[i].clone();
            Arrays.sort(currentRow);

            for (int k = 0; k < cols; k++) {
                int height = currentRow[k];
                int width = cols - k;
                maxArea = Math.max(maxArea, height * width);
            }
        }

        return maxArea;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(m⋅nlogn)
Space Complexity: O(n)




